 ## Background
-The motivation for compression arises from the quadratic or cubic scaling of NeRF parameters with respect to network depth and width. Early works like NSVF [MST*21] and TensoRF [CLBL22] explored hybrid tensor-MLP approaches. CCNeRF [S1] was introduced to provide a purely tensor-based alternative that avoids the MLP bottleneck, enabling better composition and truncation. The ADMM-based approach [S1] addresses the difficulty of directly optimizing low-rank constraints by using augmented Lagrangian methods. PackNet [ML18] was adapted to NeRF [S1] for continual learning scenarios, demonstrating that pruning can maintain performance across multiple scenes.
+PackNet was adapted to NeRF for continual learning scenarios, demonstrating that pruning can maintain performance across multiple scenes [S1]. The SCARF framework compares this with MEIL-NeRF and CLNeRF, which handle single-scene continual learning [S1]. Not given in the sources.
 
